    "explanation": "Rider and driver, the two of you agree on the route and on the accident; what is in issue is how far the accident excuses the detour and what fare follows.\n\nROUTE AND THRESHOLD. The governing distance is the GPS trace, 34.17 km (EV-GPS-00104), against the booked optimal of 27.1 km (EV-TRIP-00104). The excess is 7.07 km, or 26.09%. That passes both POL-RD-01 gates (more than 0.8 km and more than 20%), so a deviation is established. The trip record's 34.2 km is the rounded convenience copy and was not used for the test.\n\nTHE ACCIDENT. The PIE accident is genuine: LTA DataMall advisory ADV-20260716-007 (EV-ADVIS-00104-01, weight 1.0) names the PIE towards Tuas after the Eunos Link exit — the corridor the optimal route uses — and its 20:02–21:35 window covers the trip (vehicle on the road 20:35:10–21:22:10). The trace shows the vehicle leaving the PIE at Bedok North Ave 4 before the blocked stretch and rejoining, so POL-RD-03 applies and the closure-forced part of the detour is exonerated.\n\nTHE CAP AND THE REFUND. The exoneration is capped at min(7.07, 1.30 × (30.1 − 27.1) + 1.00) = 4.90 km. The closure-adjusted optimal is therefore 27.1 + 4.90 = 32.00 km, drawn at 40.1 minutes. The 34.17 km driven leaves 2.17 km beyond what the closure justifies, and POL-RD-03 provides that the excess beyond the cap is not exonerated and is refunded under POL-RD-02 computed against the closure-adjusted optimal route. The driver's argument that the 2.17 km residual is only 6.8% of 32.00 km and so must end in NO_ACTION does not survive those two clauses: the deviation stands on the trace measurement at 26.09%, and POL-RD-02 expressly directs that where POL-RD-03 'excuses part of the excess' the computation is run against the adjusted optimal — a direction that presupposes exactly this residual refund.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC. Recomputed fare = (3.80 + 0.75 × 32.00 + 0.28 × 40.1) × 1.0 (surge read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier) + 0.80 platform fee + 0.00 ERP = 39.828. Charged total 43.41. Refund = 43.41 − 39.828 = 3.582, rounded up to SGD 3.60. That is above the SGD 0.50 de minimis and below the 90% full-refund share, so this is a PARTIAL REFUND of SGD 3.60. The rider's $14 figure matches no line of the fare: even with no exoneration at all the recomputation yields only SGD 9.00, and the accident reduces the recoverable excess to the 2.17 km above.\n\nOTHER POINTS. The CTE breakdown advisory (EV-ADVIS-00104-02) is impaired (weight 0.25; ADVISORY_UNCORROBORATED and UNVERIFIED_SOURCE) and its geometry covers a corridor the trace never enters; it excuses nothing and is disregarded. POL-RD-04 does not apply: no rider message names a road or place and waypoint_hint is null on both messages, so the rider-requested-detour test cannot be run, and 'ok but not too long ah' does not excuse the route. No driver compensation is due: the fare billed 34.2 km against the 34.17 km traced, so there is no unbilled distance. No FULL REFUND uplift: the deviation is not material (below 8.0 km and 50%) and the driver shows no 90-day pattern (deviation_flags_90d 1 of 288 completed trips). No billing-integrity referral: there are no ERP gantries and no airport surcharge on this trip."
